A1. The old Maestro, Himself. ~ SIDNEYLENZ
A10. This gets the jack. ~ QUEEN
A11. Unless you know these, you'll be taken at Contract. ~ ONIONS
A14. This is the suit you play in. ~ TRUMP
A17. Four-wheeled kibitzer. ~ COACH
A19. They play for this stake in Scotland. ~ WEE
A20. To take your opponents' shirts. ~ TROUNCE
A25. A lowdown signaler. ~ TOE
A26. What wives never do to husbands' signals. ~ OBEY
A28. Unspifflicated. ~ UNLIT
A29. You'll never play with this star. ~ MIRA
A30. Bulk. ~ SIZE
A31. A little something to conjure with. ~ BUNNY
A32. I declare! ~ AVER
A33. How to give a little girl a great big hand. ~ DEAL
A35. To give a look. ~ SEE
A36. The nobility have this on their cards. ~ CREST
A37. A little tear in your best suit. ~ RIP
A38. A big club ean hand you this. ~ JAR
A39. If your trumps are this don't bid. ~ SCANT
A42. What he lost by getting up from the table. ~ LAP
A44. It's. ~ TIS
A46. An open and shut proposition in a hand. ~ PORE
A47. What bridge players fight over. ~ TABLE
A49. This always gets in the last word. ~ ECHO
A52. Greedy for tricks. ~ AVID
A53. A well-known tank. ~ OILER
A54. A misdeal in the Game of Life (slang). ~ DRIB
A55. A semi-despot (careful). ~ DES
A56. These belong on beds. ~ SPREADS
A58. Bridge player's ancestor. ~ APE
A59. Raise. ~ ERECT
A61. How a high heart makes you feel. ~ HAPPY
A63. Kibitzes. ~ SNOOPS
A66. Grandma's necking. ~ STOLE
A68. A late, initialed bridge expert. ~ WWHITEHEAD

D1. A favorite play with the ladies. ~ SQUEEZE
D2. So ends the millennium (an end play). ~ IUM
D3. The kind of store Macy's is (contracted). ~ DEPT
D4. Two famous players (init.). ~ NE
D5. Ditto, reversed. ~ EN
D6. Half a look. ~ LO
D7. Those darn players again (see above). ~ EN
D8. Where all good gamblers eventually go. ~ NICE
D9. A cagey spot. ~ ZOO
D12. Never ask directions from this. ~ NATIVE
D13. Mournful numbers. ~ SCORES
D14. This is generally wild or a member of clubs (pl.). ~ TWOS
D15. What women usually do when you take them out. ~ REBID
D16. On the spot. ~ VULNERABLE
D18. Win or lose this always beats; also, a banker's frozen asset. ~ HEART
D21. What to do to alter the score. ~ RUB
D22. You'll have to grin and bear this. ~ ONUS
D23. When you get by this you're in the honor class. ~ NINE
D24. A blind city. ~ CTY
D27. All bridge players have done this for a game. ~ YEARNED
D29. Never play with opponents who are this way. ~ MARRIED
D34. Well oiled. ~ LIT
D36. What it takes to look at a queen. ~ CAT
D39. This belongs with the black hand. ~ SPADE
D40. The king usually does this to the queen. ~ COVERS
D41. Gotten up in the world. ~ ARISEN
D42. Dora thinks this is the kind of cake you get in the jungle. ~ LAIR
D43. An S.O.S. ~ PLEA
D45. What the pot-wrastler did for a living. ~ SCRAPED
D47. You'll have a circus under this. ~ TOP
D48. This herd has no head. ~ ERD
D50. How the hippopotamus began. ~ HIPP
D51. Marriage warning to women (obsolete). ~ OBEY
D56. Pack away. ~ STOW
D57. A little kid under the table. ~ SHOE
D60. This taught the farmer the squeeze play. ~ COW
D62. The mode follows this. ~ ALA
D64. This stands for Punk Hands. ~ PH
D65. How to start signaling. ~ SI
D66. Another pair of those darn players (init.). ~ SE
D67. Thith thtants for an eth. ~ TH


Misprints, kept as printed (printed → meant):
D67: Thith thtants for an eth. → thtandth (a lisped 'stands'; the lisp is the joke, printed cleanly as 'thtants')

Warnings:
Grid isn't symmetric: 2 square pair(s) differ (r3c8/r13c8, r7c2/r9c14). Check those black squares against the scan, or confirm the design is asymmetric.

Printed with puzzle:
This week we present the Cross Bridge Work Puzzle, which is not a matter of dentistry, but simply the happy marriage of this dept. and Mr. Lenz' dept. The definitions are mostly taken from the bridge table but we feel sure that none of them is too technical even for the bridge player.

